# KNOWN GAP — 🚨 THE SAME PROGRAM IN THREE SPELLINGS; THE ONE THAT MUTATES
# THROUGH AN UNNAMED GET-CHAIN TEMP SEGFAULTS.
#
#     Vector[String] first = outer.get(0).unwrap()
#     String s = first.get(0).unwrap()      # view into the inner vector
#     ...
#     first.push(...)                       # -> rc 0, "hello"
#     outer[0].push(...)                    # -> rc 0, "hello"
#     outer.get(0).unwrap().push(...)       # -> rc 139 SIGSEGV
#
# All three push to the SAME inner vector and are semantics-equivalent. Only
# the spelling of the MUTATED RECEIVER differs, and it decides whether the
# program segfaults.
#
#     C backend        : SIGSEGV, rc 139
#     C + --sanitize   : AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free in
#                        `gorget_string_copy_cow`
#     LLVM backend     : SIGSEGV, rc 139
#     control spellings: rc 0, `hello`, on BOTH backends
#
# ⚠ DISCRIMINATOR — this is NOT `t0699` and NOT `t0703`, and the difference
# is what makes it a separate cell rather than a third costume:
#   * `t0699` -- the query carries the RIGHT key to the WRONG SET (a user
#     `&self` mutator's name is absent from the 25-entry `MUTATING_METHODS`
#     list). Detection fails on the SET.
#   * `t0703` -- the query carries the WRONG KEY (the spelling `alias`) to
#     the right set. Detection fails on the KEY.
#   * THIS -- there is NO KEY TO CARRY. The mutated receiver is an unnamed
#     temporary produced by a get-chain, so `CollectionId` has no name to
#     resolve and no spelling to union. The predicate has NO SUBJECT for this
#     shape at all -- a category error, not a missing entry. No widening of
#     the name set and no union of spellings can reach it, because both
#     operate on names and this position never has one.
#     (AGENTS.md Core #15e Q4: "is there a case with no subject at all?")
#
# ⚠ THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING: the inner vector must be pushed enough to
# force its backing store to reallocate (64x here) and the payload must be
# heap-FORCED via `mk`, never a literal. Fewer pushes or a literal payload is
# rc 0 on every spelling and proves nothing.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints `hello` on both backends, ASan-clean,
# regardless of which of the three equivalent spellings does the mutation.
#
# RED-verified 2026-08-27 by the R45 orchestrator: built and run on BOTH
# lanes at HEAD (rc 139 each) with the two control spellings run alongside
# (rc 0, `hello`, each) and the ASan report captured.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[Vector[String]] outer = []
    Vector[String] inner = []
    inner.push(mk("hel", "lo"))
    outer.push(inner)
    Vector[String] first = outer.get(0).unwrap()
    String s = first.get(0).unwrap()
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        outer.get(0).unwrap().push(mk("fillfillfillfill", "ererererererer"))
        i = i + 1
    print(s)
